  "body": [
    "The dashboard reads as reflation with an inflationary edge. Copper sits at the top of its 52-week range, up 4% over the month and 19% year to date, with volatility unusually calm — a confident growth signal, not a squeeze. Against it, the 10-year yield has pushed to a one-year high at 4.76%, up 28 basis points over three months. Growth expectations and inflation pressure are rising together, and the bond market is repricing accordingly.",
    "Risk appetite confirms the read. Bitcoin is up 25% over the month and now buys 9% more gold than a month ago — the pattern of liquidity and speculative confidence, not fear. Gold's own story cuts the same way: its 11% monthly gain alongside rising yields looks like an inflation hedge rather than a safety bid, and its 4% pullback this week while risk assets held suggests the fear component is fading. The dollar is broadly flat over the month, so none of this is a currency illusion.",
    "The one tension is the long end: the 30-year has lagged, narrowing the 30y–10y spread by 4 basis points over the month. That concentrates the repricing in the medium term — the market is marking up near-horizon inflation and policy risk more than distant fiscal worry. Softer Brent over the week and month takes some edge off, but the copper-yields-bitcoin configuration carries more weight."
